{"id":"b24eeae9-410c-4e54-86ba-2ab8737b910d","object":"chat.completion","created":1784990901,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"import json\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    tokens = case['tokens']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    # Validate required fields\\n    for c in calls:\\n        if not all(k in c for k in ('call_id', 'start', 'end', 'score', 'replacement_tokens')):\\n            raise ValueError('Missing required field in call')\\n        if not isinstance(c['start'], int) or not isinstance(c['end'], int):\\n            raise ValueError('start and end must be integers')\\n        if c['start'] < 0 or c['end'] > len(tokens):\\n            raise ValueError('start/end indices out of range')\\n    # Sort candidates: descending score, then ascending start, then call_id\\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda x: (-x['score'], x['start'], x['call_id']))\\n    selected = []\\n    for c in sorted_calls:\\n        s, e = c['start'], c['end']\\n        # Check overlap with already selected\\n        overlap = False\\n        for sel in selected:\\n            if not (e <= sel['start'] or s >= sel['end']):\\n                overlap = True\\n                break\\n        if not overlap:\\n            selected.append(c)\\n    # Order selected by start for reporting\\n    selected_sorted = sorted(selected, key=lambda x: x['start'])\\n    selected_ids = [c['call_id'] for c in selected_sorted]\\n    # Apply replacements right to left to preserve offsets\\n    result_tokens = list(tokens)\\n    for c in sorted(selected, key=lambda x: -x['start']):\\n        s, e = c['start'], c['end']\\n        repl = c['replacement_tokens']\\n        result_tokens = result_tokens[:s] + repl + result_tokens[e:]\\n    return {\\n        'selected_call_ids': selected_ids,\\n        'tokens': result_tokens\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write solve(case) that takes a payload with \"tokens\" (list of strings) and \"calls\" (list of dicts each with call_id, start, end, score, replacement_tokens). We need to select calls without overlap, order by descending score, then start, then call_id. Then we need to produce interleaved token stream: apply replacements from right to left to avoid offset shifts. Also return selected_call_ids (list of call_ids in order of start? Actually \"Order accepted calls by source start for reporting.\" So selected_call_ids should be sorted by start position. And tokens output is the final interleaved token stream after applying replacements.\n\nLet's outline algorithm:\n1. Parse calls list.\n2. Filter calls: ensure each has required fields? Probably assume valid, but we can validate. For each call, check start, end (ints), score (float), replacement_tokens (list of strings), call_id (string). Also start inclusive, end exclusive? In example: start 1 end 3 covers indices 1,2; start 2 end 5 covers 2,3,4; those overlap. We need to reject overlapping spans based on greedy selection by score descending, then start, then call_id. Keep selected list.\n3. Sort candidates by (-score, start, call_id) for selection.\n4. Greedily select: iterate sorted candidates, for each, check if it overlaps with any already selected call. Overlap: intervals [start, end) and [s, e) overlap if max(start, s) < min(end, e). If no overlap, add to selected set (store call_id and full data).\n5. After selection, sort selected by start (ascending) for output order.\n6. To produce tokens: start with copy of original tokens list. Apply replacements from right to left (i.e., sort selected by start descending). For each selected call, replace tokens[start:end] with its replacement_tokens list. This is a list of strings, so we need to replace slice with list. In Python: tokens = tokens[:start] + call['replacement_tokens'] + tokens[end:]. Since we do right to left, indices remain valid.\n7. Return {\"selected_call_ids\": list of call_ids sorted by start, \"tokens\": final tokens list}.\n\nEdge cases: overlapping? Already handled. No calls? Return empty list and original tokens. Ensure we don't mutate original tokens list.\n\nCheck fixture: For case 000, tokens 0..9. Calls: a(1-3), b(2-5), c(7-9). Sorted: b score 0.9, a score 0.8, c score 0.7. Start: b 2, a 1, c 7. tie break: b first, then a. Check overlap with b selected: a overlaps (1-3 vs 2-5). So a rejected. c does not overlap with b (7-9 vs 2-5) so selected. So selected: b and c. Order by start: b(2) then c(7). Apply replacements right to left: c first: replace indices 7-9 with ['<c>'] -> tokens become: t0-t6, <c>, t9 (since end=9, slice t9 excluded? Actually tokens[7:9] = [t7, t8] replaced by ['<c>'] -> length changes from 2 to 1). Then b: replace indices 2-5 with ['<b>'] -> tokens[2:5] = [t2,t3,t4] replaced by ['<b>'] -> length changes from 3 to 1. Final tokens: t0, t1, '<b>', t5, t6, '<c>', t9? Wait careful: after first replacement (c): tokens = ['t0','t1','t2','t3','t4','t5','t6','<c>','t9'] (since t7,t8 removed, t9 stays at index 8). Then apply b: tokens[2:5] = ['t2','t3','t4'] replaced by ['<b>'] -> tokens become: ['t0','t1','<b>','t5','t6','<c>','t9']. That's 7 tokens. That seems plausible.\n\nNow implement solve(case). Use only standard library.\n\nNeed to output a JSON string with key \"implementation\" and value the Python source as a JSON string. So wrap in json.dumps.\n\nWe'll write:\n\nimport json\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # assume case has tokens and calls\n    tokens = case['tokens']\n    calls = case['calls']\n    # Validate required fields? Not required per spec but can raise ValueError if missing.\n    # Selection\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (-c['score'], c['start'], c['call_id']))\n    selected = []\n    # intervals of selected calls\n    for c in sorted_calls:\n        overlap = False\n        s, e = c['start'], c['end']\n        for sel in selected:\n            if not (e <= sel['start'] or s >= sel['end']):\n                overlap = True\n                break\n        if not overlap:\n            selected.append(c)\n    # Sort selected by start for output\n    selected_sorted_by_start = sorted(selected, key=lambda c: c['start'])\n    selected_ids = [c['call_id'] for c in selected_sorted_by_start]\n    # Apply replacements right-to-left\n    result_tokens = list(tokens)  # copy\n    # sort by start descending\n    for c in sorted(selected, key=lambda c: -c['start']):\n        s, e = c['start'], c['end']\n        repl = c['replacement_tokens']\n        result_tokens = result_tokens[:s] + repl + result_tokens[e:]\n    return {\n        \"selected_call_ids\": selected_ids,\n        \"tokens\": result_tokens\n    }\n\nBut careful: after applying a replacement on the right, the list changes. But since we go from high start to low start, earlier indices are not affected. That's correct.\n\nWe must also handle case where start/end indices might be out of range? But fixtures are valid. If invalid, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso note that replacement_tokens may be empty list? That's allowed: replace slice with empty list. So we handle.\n\nNow produce final output: {\"implementation\": \"<python source as JSON string>\"}. Use json.dumps to properly escape.\n\nWe need to ensure the function doesn't add any extra imports beyond standard library. We'll import json at top of the implementation string. But the solve function itself may use json? Not needed because return is dict. So fine.\n\nDouble-check output contract: must return exactly {\"selected_call_ids\": list of strings, \"tokens\": list of strings}. Tokens are strings. Yes.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1654,"completion_tokens":1947,"total_tokens":3601,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1536},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1487},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1536,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":118},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}